Exactly. Those are two different skills.

Transmute it when the energy is useful but the original expression is not. Anger can become boundaries, competition, training, sex, humor, work, protection, decisiveness, art. You keep the charge and change its vector.

Let it go when even carrying the charge is costing more than it is giving you. No lesson, no victory, no conversion required. You stop feeding it attention and let the state decay.

And there’s a third move between them: integrate it. Sometimes the feeling is carrying information—hurt, fear, jealousy, humiliation, exhaustion, unmet need. You neither act it out nor discard it immediately; you understand what it is telling you, take the useful information, and release the rest.

So the sequence can be very simple:

Feel it → read it → choose: use it, integrate it, or release it.

That is more freedom than being either “the fighter” or “the lover.” It means neither state owns you. You can choose the form.